{
  "id": "how-many-stars",
  "title": "How Many Stars?",
  "level": "A1",
  "summary": "Look out of the window at night. How many things can you count in the sky?",
  "teachingMethod": "Theme-based",
  "teachingMethodReason": "Theme-based rather than CLIL: a single theme (the night sky) organizes the vocabulary and recycles one fixed language pattern (\"There is / There are...\") across every page. That is the organizing thread the course's theme-based unit describes - topic-led vocabulary grouping with a repeated structure. It is NOT CLIL, because no subject content is actually taught: the book never explains why the moon is round or what a star is made of, and asks for no cause-and-effect reasoning (compare zoe-animal-homes, which teaches habitats). It is not story-based either - nothing happens, there is no problem and no resolution.",
  "grammarFeatures": [
    "\"There is\" and \"There are\" for saying what exists",
    "Numbers with singular and plural nouns (one moon, two clouds)",
    "Descriptive adjectives (round, grey, tall)",
    "\"Can\" in questions (Can you count them?)"
  ],
  "introFunFacts": [
    "🔢 This book is full of counting! Point at each thing and count out loud as you read.",
    "1️⃣ 'There IS one moon' but 'There ARE two clouds' - we say 'are' when there is more than one!",
    "🌙 Read this one at bedtime, then look out of your own window and count what YOU can see."
  ],
